WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:00.953
- What's up, y'all?
(audience applauding)

00:00:00.953 --> 00:00:02.903
I'm back with Jessie Ennis, give it up!

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:05.580
I love your dress, by the way,

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:06.462
and I want it.
- [Jessie] Thank you!

00:00:06.462 --> 00:00:08.980
- I hope my stylist is
looking at you right now.

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:11.740
Getting in trouble with
the law at an early age

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:14.860
can lead to a lifetime
of consequences for kids.

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:17.710
My next guest is looking to
change all that in Seattle

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:20.970
with an innovative program
that gives kids an alternative.

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:22.240
Instead of being prosecuted,

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:25.320
they can choose to see the
best in themselves and thrive.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.480
Take a look.
(air whooshes)

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.670
- CHOOSE 180 as a nonprofit organization.

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:32.350
Our foremost goal is to
transform systems of injustice

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:35.080
to no longer do harm to communities,

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:36.720
particularly, the black
and brown community.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:38.130
- So tell me about a time

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:39.920
that you made that different decision.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:41.210
- [Sean] The difference
between a young person

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:42.180
who's successful,

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:44.020
and a young person who has challenges,

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:46.142
has everything to do with the
community surrounding them.

00:00:46.142 --> 00:00:46.980
(slapping sound)

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:48.700
- What the program aims to do is

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:51.170
to not label a kid for a mistake.

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:54.240
It seeks to help the kid
make better decisions.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.100
It doesn't always have to be a jail cell,

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.390
or a judge, or a courtroom.

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:00.747
- Last year, we supported
a little over 500 youth

00:01:00.747 --> 00:01:02.160
and young adults.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:05.130
Over 90% of them don't return
to the criminal legal system

00:01:05.130 --> 00:01:08.090
within 12 months of
participating in our program.

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:09.460
Which means that community is better

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:10.780
because a young person is better,

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:12.602
because that family is better.

00:01:12.602 --> 00:01:14.976
(audience applauds)

00:01:14.976 --> 00:01:15.809
(air whooshes)

00:01:15.809 --> 00:01:17.500
- This is the greatest organization.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:18.480
I'm so excited y'all.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.070
Over the years, Sean Goode,

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:22.330
the executive director
of CHOOSE 180 has helped

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:25.960
more than 6,000 kids
turn their lives around.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:28.234
He's what we like to call a "Rad Human."

00:01:28.234 --> 00:01:30.817
(lively music)

00:01:33.006 --> 00:01:33.839
- Woo!
(Kelly applauds)

00:01:33.839 --> 00:01:36.422
Please welcome Sean, everybody!

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:38.267
What's up, man?

00:01:38.267 --> 00:01:39.360
How you doing?

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:41.570
- I am amazing, Kelly.

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:42.940
- Well, meet Jessie.

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:43.980
- Hi, nice to meet you.

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:45.440
- Hey Jessie.
- [Jessie] Hi.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:46.950
- Great to meet you as well.
- [Jessie] You too.

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:48.340
- Well we were just talking in the break,

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:50.020
how excited we are, like,
she does this podcast.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:51.530
She gets to meet people
that are really cool

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:52.580
and changing people's lives

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.130
and have a lot of information

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:55.210
that we, maybe, don't know about.

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:59.410
And I have been so excited
since reading all about you,

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:00.340
about having you on the show.

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:02.890
I think it's incredible what you're doing.

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:06.280
So, two very different
events in your childhood

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:08.600
inspired what you do right now, right?

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:09.433
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:02:09.433 --> 00:02:12.200
You know, when I was six years old,

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:15.080
I visited my brother,
who, at the age of 13,

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:18.050
was incarcerated for a
poor choice that he made

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:20.470
and he was locked up inside of a kid jail

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:23.350
until he was a 21-year-old man.

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:25.210
And when I went to visit him on this day,

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:28.540
the security guard, when I
stepped into the building,

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:30.810
pointed me to an article that
was on the wall behind me.

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:33.300
And this article was all about my brother

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.370
and how he did this amazing job going

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:38.170
and visiting a school and
inspiring the young people.

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:39.980
And it wasn't lost on me at the age of six

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:41.830
that, although he could do so much great

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:43.440
outside of this building,

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.110
because of a poor choice he
made when he was a 13-year-old,

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:48.720
he was stuck in here until he was 21.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.980
And by the time I got to
the age of about 10, 11, 12,

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:54.560
I had a really difficult
anger management problems.

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:57.240
And I would break holes
and doors and walls

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:00.110
and even harm my mother and
the police got called on me.

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:01.500
And I remember this day clearly,

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.060
when the police brought me down

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:04.597
and put me in the back
of their car and said,

00:03:04.597 --> 00:03:07.640
"Look, we can take you with
us or send you back upstairs.

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:10.650
But if we send you back upstairs,
and we have to come back,

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:12.880
you're definitely coming with us again."

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:15.800
And that moment of grace was
a big pivot moment for me

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:18.300
that helped me commit to a
new direction for my life.

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:19.140
- Wow!

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:21.840
And that's quite a moment
too, because you never know

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:24.260
you know, what the kid
will choose, you know,

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:26.440
and it's an interesting thing though,

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:27.600
that you've pointed out,

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:30.250
that they can choose the positive path.

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:33.110
And I think that's the coolest
part of what you're doing.

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:35.320
The kids in CHOOSE 180, just have to do

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:37.970
a one-day program to get
their charges dropped, right?

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:39.440
So how does it work?

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:41.080
- We have this really unique relationship

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:43.270
with the district
attorneys in our community

00:03:43.270 --> 00:03:45.050
where we're able to give
young people community

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:46.220
instead of a courtroom.

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:48.160
And, essentially, what happens
is when a young person's

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:51.620
behavior is criminalized, they
get referred to our program

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:53.140
instead of referred to the court system.

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:55.470
They show up to the
shared space with people

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:57.550
with lived experience that mirrors them.

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:00.160
They hear testimonies from
folks through this exercise

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:01.750
we have called the
"Color of Connectedness,"

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:03.890
where, I'll tell my story, for example,

00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:06.840
about how I used to damage
property that wasn't mine.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:09.020
And then, I will invite the young people,

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:11.420
they're connected adults,
the prosecutors in the space,

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:12.700
the staff, the volunteers.

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:15.480
So I'll come down and get
like a green Mardi Gras bead

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:17.570
and we'll do the same thing
for physical violence,

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:19.150
for theft, and drugs, and alcohol.

00:04:19.150 --> 00:04:21.210
And before you know it,
everybody in the space

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:22.960
has these beads around their neck

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:24.300
and the young people recognize

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:26.000
that I'm not here because I'm guilty,

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:28.020
'cause if that's the case,
then all of us are guilty.

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:29.630
Then it must be for something different.

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:30.463
It's for grace.

00:04:30.463 --> 00:04:32.550
And we've recognized that
grace is a far better motivator

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:34.630
for behavior change than
guilt ever could be.

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:36.320
- Absolutely.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:37.910
Did you feel like you
had moments in your life

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:41.180
that you're like, "Oh, God,
I'm glad that my whole path

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:43.470
wasn't decided because
of that one thing, like,

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:44.303
one mistake."

00:04:44.303 --> 00:04:45.800
- Yeah, yeah, I mean, what you're saying,

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:49.070
it really reminds me of this
thing that I try to recognize,

00:04:49.070 --> 00:04:51.110
which is that, like,
there's angels everywhere.

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:54.640
And so, my story is
when I was a little kid,

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:55.810
I didn't really like school that much,

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:57.350
but I knew I wanted to be an actor.

00:04:57.350 --> 00:04:59.550
And my parents sent me
to this acting program

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:00.580
over the summer.

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:02.350
And then the following semester,

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:04.610
we hadn't enrolled because
my family was tight on money

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:06.380
and we just couldn't afford to swing it.

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:08.210
And the director of the
program called my parents

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:10.860
and be like, "Hey, we noticed
that Jessie isn't signed up

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:11.760
for this next semester.

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:13.320
Just trying to, you know,

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:14.950
make sure that you're all good to go.

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:16.170
Like it's starting soon."

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:17.570
And my mom explained, "Oh, you know,

00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:18.600
we're a little tight right now.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:20.880
So we're gonna take a little time off.

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:23.967
And this director of
the program, she said,

00:05:23.967 --> 00:05:26.940
"Well, you know, Jessie
is really intense in class

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:28.650
and she gets the other kids to focus.

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:31.580
So, we wanna offer her
a lifetime scholarship

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:33.390
and she can come whenever she wants."

00:05:33.390 --> 00:05:35.810
And so, I got to go to
acting classes for 10 years

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:37.340
because of this woman Ellen Mendel.

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:38.620
And it was like-
- [Kelly] Wow!

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:40.430
- totally blessed by an
angel kind of moment.

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:42.270
I'm so grateful to her.
- You know, it's so funny.

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:43.780
I had similar moments in my life.

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:44.970
We had no money.

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:46.840
There wasn't a lot of support in that area

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:48.000
'cause there just couldn't be.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:49.400
And I had people help me.

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:50.920
And I think what you're saying is,

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:53.530
if you give kids that maybe
don't have the opportunity,

00:05:53.530 --> 00:05:56.050
because of finances they
don't have, the opportunity,

00:05:56.050 --> 00:05:58.010
because of, they don't
have support system,

00:05:58.010 --> 00:06:01.500
those little moments, look
at where we are right now.

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:04.890
We would probably not be here
had people not believed in us

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:06.900
and had they not, you know,

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:09.050
kind of helped us on a better path,

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:11.180
and one that we'd had opportunity.

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:13.490
A few years ago, our
next guest was homeless

00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:15.370
and facing serious jail time.

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:17.270
That's when she discovered CHOOSE 180

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:19.090
and made a commitment to change.

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:21.440
Now, her life looks drastically different.

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:23.203
Everybody, say hello to Lamaria!

00:06:24.058 --> 00:06:25.289
(audience applauds)

00:06:25.289 --> 00:06:27.247
Hi, Lamaria!
- Hi!

00:06:27.247 --> 00:06:29.501
- Hi, say hi to Jessie, as well.

00:06:29.501 --> 00:06:30.810
- Hi!

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:33.010
- Hi, Jessie!
(Kelly giggles)

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:33.870
- All right, well, Lamaria,

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:36.520
can you tell us a little
about your background first?

00:06:37.390 --> 00:06:40.970
- You know, I grew up and
I was bullied, you know,

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:43.030
being a bigger girl,

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:45.660
my mom wasn't financially stable to get me

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:50.660
the things that could distract
bullies from bullying me.

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:55.420
So, at about 13, my behavior
changed and I began stealing.

00:06:55.420 --> 00:06:58.070
I didn't enter the criminal justice system

00:06:58.070 --> 00:06:59.943
until I was 18, for theft.

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:03.860
And, when I went to jail, I got out,

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:05.750
I got out to being homeless, Kelly.

00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:09.890
I got out to couch surfing, you know,

00:07:09.890 --> 00:07:12.187
and I was entering into survival mode.

00:07:12.187 --> 00:07:13.410
"How can I survive?"

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:16.290
And that led to cashing bad checks

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:21.290
and cashing bad checks led to
facing seven felony forgeries.

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:25.850
So, you can only imagine,
you know, how that was.

00:07:25.850 --> 00:07:28.440
- I think that's the interesting
thing about situations

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:30.510
like this, is everybody
thinks they're invincible.

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:32.600
You're not, you don't have any idea, like,

00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:34.090
what could happen, your
place could burn down.

00:07:34.090 --> 00:07:35.340
Everything can go up in flames.

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:36.290
And, all of a sudden,

00:07:36.290 --> 00:07:37.990
you're in a completely
different environment,

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:40.160
or you don't have the support around you.

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:42.100
You don't have people to call for money,

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:43.650
and, like, not everybody has that.

00:07:43.650 --> 00:07:45.530
And that can happen to anyone.

00:07:45.530 --> 00:07:48.180
Well, what was your experience
like with the program?

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:51.593
- Oh, my experience was amazing, Kelly.

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:55.370
You know, like I said
earlier, I was homeless.

00:07:55.370 --> 00:07:57.320
I was in a place where I didn't know

00:07:57.320 --> 00:07:58.420
if I could stop stealing.

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:01.765
I didn't know if I could
stop cashing checks.

00:08:01.765 --> 00:08:05.350
And, I came into the program,
I walked into the workshop,

00:08:05.350 --> 00:08:07.460
thinking it's going to be a one day thing,

00:08:07.460 --> 00:08:09.730
but it turned into two years and a half

00:08:09.730 --> 00:08:13.770
of turning my life around,
building amazing relationships.

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:18.430
And I, in that, in the midst
of that time, I got stable.

00:08:18.430 --> 00:08:20.650
I learned life lessons.

00:08:20.650 --> 00:08:25.580
How to financially save, how
to be stable within a home.

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:28.850
They paid for all my moving costs.

00:08:28.850 --> 00:08:31.313
Just, just a new start for me.

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:34.850
It was amazing, honestly, truthfully,

00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:37.393
and in the midst of that as well,

00:08:38.270 --> 00:08:41.847
all seven forgery
charges, Kelly, are gone.

00:08:41.847 --> 00:08:42.680
(Lamaria laughs)
(audience applauds)

00:08:42.680 --> 00:08:44.570
I did not have to go to jail.

00:08:44.570 --> 00:08:48.190
There were so many community
leaders that, on my behalf,

00:08:48.190 --> 00:08:50.067
wrote a letter that, you know,

00:08:50.067 --> 00:08:53.580
"Lamaria, you know, was in
a circumstances to survive

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:54.870
and that's not her.

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:56.630
She can do better."

00:08:56.630 --> 00:08:59.640
And that resulted to employment.

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:03.050
Sean just offered me
employment to give back

00:09:03.050 --> 00:09:05.470
to my community, to help out youth,

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:07.320
young girls just like me.

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:09.000
So, that's my calling.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:11.380
And I just wanna be the best at that.

00:09:11.380 --> 00:09:12.213
- I love it.

00:09:12.213 --> 00:09:13.230
You wanna be the best version of yourself.

00:09:13.230 --> 00:09:14.650
And that's what I love from the video too,

00:09:14.650 --> 00:09:16.870
that we played before we brought Sean out,

00:09:16.870 --> 00:09:20.170
is, you know, he said, "This
isn't just a good thing

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:21.050
for these individuals.

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:22.540
It's an amazing thing
for these individuals.

00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:23.750
But it's an even bigger thing

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:26.650
and in a bigger impact on
the community as a whole."

00:09:26.650 --> 00:09:28.680
Lamaria, I understand
that you have something

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:30.143
you wanna say to Sean, right?

00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:32.830
- Yes, I do.

00:09:32.830 --> 00:09:34.810
Uncle Sean, I just wanted to let you know,

00:09:34.810 --> 00:09:37.470
thank you for everything
you've done for me.

00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:41.020
Thank you for everything you
do for the Seattle community.

00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:43.310
I love you forever for it.

00:09:43.310 --> 00:09:45.170
And I appreciate all the opportunities

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:47.326
and I can't wait to see
what the future holds.

00:09:47.326 --> 00:09:48.159
(audience applauds)

00:09:48.159 --> 00:09:50.460
- And we love what CHOOSE 180 is all about

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:52.110
and how it's changing lives.

00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:56.470
So, Sean, we are giving
the program $10,000.

00:09:56.470 --> 00:09:58.600
How's that sound?
- What?!

00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:00.140
Oh, that sounds amazing, Kelly!

00:10:00.140 --> 00:10:02.130
(Kelly laughs and applauds)

00:10:02.130 --> 00:10:03.630
- All right!

00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:05.010
We love what you're doing

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:06.820
and I've been so excited, literally,

00:10:06.820 --> 00:10:08.140
to have you on this show.

